leticia variants eldest grandson alexis  is sick with dengue fever and has been  in this public hospital for several  weeks now but he isn't the only one  across the hall her two other grandsons  are also being treated for a measles  infection six-year-old Prince ander and  four year-old James Mateo she says she  is somehow grateful that her  grandchildren made it to the hospital in  time it has been very difficult seeing  my grandsons like this but I have no  choice I have to be strong san lazaro  hospital is just one of the many  hospitals where patriotic wards have  been overflowing with children sick with  infectious diseases the Department of  Health has recorded at least 40,000  cases of measles across the country  since January and more than 500 patients  have died while the cases of those with  dengue have reached more than 270,000  with more than a thousand 100 dead many  of those who died were children but it  doesn't end there the government has  announced the return of polio type 2 an  incurable disease that was eradicated 19  years ago public trust on vaccine has  plummeted according to the global  vaccine confidence index from 93 percent  now down to 32 percent which means now  that only one out of five Filipinos  believe that vaccines are safe the  health secretary says a recent  investigation into a vaccination  campaign against dengue fever by the  public Attorney's Office is partly to  blame for the falling vaccination rates  this kind of issue is best discussed in  a scientific forum by scientists doctors  and professionals and should not be in a  Senate hearing or on television with  people who are uninformed and educated  about the issue making definitive  comments the Philippine government says  it promises to roll out a series of  nationwide immunization programs but  back at the intensive care unit of San  lázaro hospital every second counts  there's a sense of urgency here and  parents are desperate for their children  to recover  Chameli Logan al-jazeera Manila
 

